<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Big Tree</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Big Tree was signed in 1797 at Geneseo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, between the Seneca and the financier Robert Morris. The Seneca sold their rights to most of their land west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genesee-river">Genesee River</a> for 100,000 dollars, keeping only a number of reservations. The sale opened western New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> to settlement through the Holland Land Company.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-big-tree.jpg" alt="Treaty of Big Tree" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dougtone · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Livingston_County,_New_York_-_7596281114.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
